{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>An ox or a sheep.</b> After He mentioned every kind of blemish in consecrated offerings and removed them from the altar — even though at times the unblemished animal may be worth one shekel and the blemished one two, on account of its size and fatness, and at times a blemish may be considered a delicacy for a king of flesh and blood — this is because הַצּוּר תָּמִים פָּעֳלוֹ (Deuteronomy 32:4) desires the perfection and completeness of both the offering and the one who offers: that the offering be upon its natural perfection and the offerer upon his divine perfection, to resemble his Maker as far as possible. He said that likewise regarding the bounds of the times that He set for it, one may neither add nor subtract. And He mentioned the prohibition of an animal lacking its required time, the prohibition of slaughtering it and its son on one day, and the prohibition of offering outside its appointed time even in minor consecrations. And He mentioned the thanksgiving offering, for although it belongs to the category of peace offerings, its time is limited to one day and one night only, and not two days and a night as with other peace offerings."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:37:22.507389273Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}